      {
        "id": 266093,
        "name": "",
        "username": "fjur",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/fjur/{size}/4849_2.png",
        "created_at": "2026-08-31T23:39:43.777Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Chief_Lackey_Rich\" data-post=\"20\" data-topic=\"32522\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/6de8d8/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Chief_Lackey_Rich:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>There are so few canon ways to threaten Clark (or any Kryptonian, really) that some writers just keep going back to a few things - Kryptonite, red sun radiation, magic, threatening the supporting cast - for lack of any other easy levers to work with.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I guess. I never really fully bought that argument. Like, Supes or Legs or whoever aren’t omnipotent—it’s just a matter of scale. You don’t need to turn off their powers to threaten them, just give them a threat of an appropriate level. Superman has plenty of foes who are, more or less, just as strong as him. Legacy had Iron Curtain, I guess; he could use more punchier foes—well, really more foes in general. Plus, you can always have ways to threaten the heroes that are lateral to their powers—all the superstrength and bulletproof skin don’t do anything against Miss Information’s reality warping or Mr. Jitters’ frighteningness.</p>\n<p>Also, I do feel like the <em>Superman</em> (2025) film had a pretty clever spin on the classic kryptonite moment: <span class=\"spoiler\">instead of just heroically willpowering though it or whatever, Supes essentially talked the kryptonite down. (It makes sense in context.)</span></p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Chief_Lackey_Rich\" data-post=\"20\" data-topic=\"32522\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/6de8d8/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Chief_Lackey_Rich:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>C&amp;A still decided to introduce regression serum to have a simple Kryptonite analog.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>The RS is different from kryptonite, though, in that although sometimes Lex and others can synthesise it, often it’s a rare substance, but there’s never been any mention of any limiting factor for Blade using RS. He seems to essentially be able to have it on him whenever he wants to.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Chief_Lackey_Rich\" data-post=\"20\" data-topic=\"32522\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/6de8d8/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Chief_Lackey_Rich:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Ah well, at least it got us Pink Kryptonite - although I don’t think we’ve seen what (if anything) it does to Kara.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>According to the DC Wiki, Kara <em>has</em> been exposed to Pink K—but in that story, its effect was just to make her fall in love with some random dude.</p>",
