      {
        "id": 266094,
        "name": "Chief Lackey Rich",
        "username": "Chief_Lackey_Rich",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/6de8d8/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2026-09-01T02:52:28.074Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"fjur\" data-post=\"21\" data-topic=\"32522\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/fjur/48/4849_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> fjur:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>According to the DC Wiki, Kara <em>has</em> been exposed to Pink K—but in that story, its effect was just to make her fall in love with some random dude.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Well, that’s disappointing.</p>\n<p>Unless he was secretly a part-time horse/centaur.  <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/slight_smile.png?v=12\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>\n<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"fjur\" data-post=\"21\" data-topic=\"32522\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/fjur/48/4849_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> fjur:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Like, Supes or Legs or whoever aren’t omnipotent—it’s just a matter of scale. You don’t need to turn off their powers to threaten them, just give them a threat of an appropriate level.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>That can work in a comic, but it doesn’t do so well for RPG purposes.  I think we’re all familiar with power imbalances between PCs leading to one (or more) characters who require such high-powered foes to be a straightforward threat that a bruising hit to the party tank would one-shot most or all of the other PCs.  It’s especially an issue in most supers RPGs (although Sentinels handles it better than most) but it happens even in other genres.  That one Traveller dude in battle dress calls for the opposition to have guns that will outright kill some poor chump in ballistic mesh just from the collateral plasma splash.</p>\n<p>I remeber fiddling around with Champions way back when where one powerhouse hero had most of his powers bought with a limitation that they couldn’t exceed the active points of whoever they were contending with, so his attacks and defenses scaled up and down constantly depending on who/what he was attacking or being attacked by.  Never did quite work, but it did sort of solve the massive power imbalance issues.  More importantly, it fit his theme of being “Mister Fair Play” and sticking to measured responses to violence.</p>",
