        "username": "fjur",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/fjur/{size}/4849_2.png",
        "created_at": "2026-09-01T18:38:29.830Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Sure, team balance, in both games and stories, is a related but distinct issue from the kryptonite thing. I agree that the SCRPG does handle it pretty well; here, Superman and Wildcat can both have <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/uploads/default/original/2X/2/25f0d022a48321f60d425e1534e706ee824a4b1b.png?v=12\" title=\":d10:\" class=\"emoji emoji-custom\" alt=\":d10:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"> for instance, Wildcat is just more restricted in who he can punch. And sometimes it is addressed in comics, as well. I recall, in the novelisation of <em>Crisis on Infinite Earths</em> (I don’t recall if the original comic had it), despite Batman being absent from one of the big attacks against the Anti-Monitor, the Flash notes that he recognises the strategy being used as Bats’. So even if a hero isn’t powerful enough to directly punch or blast or whatever a threat, they can likely still contribute in other ways.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Chief_Lackey_Rich\" data-post=\"22\" data-topic=\"32522\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/6de8d8/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Chief_Lackey_Rich:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>More importantly, it fit his theme of being “Mister Fair Play” and sticking to measured responses to violence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I don’t suppose he happened to have a cool jacket proclaiming his adherence to that ideal? <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/slight_smile.png?v=12\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"></p>",
